course: medicine
lesson: 3
title: Sprains, strains and bruises
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sprain is a joint injury: the ligaments, strong straps that hold bones together, get stretched or torn a little, like when you twist your ankle in football.
explain: A strain is a muscle injury: some muscle fibres are overstretched, often when you sprint before warming up.
explain: A bruise is blood leaking from tiny broken vessels under the skin, and it changes colour, purple, blue, green, yellow, as the body cleans it up.
explain: In the first day or two, rest, something cold wrapped in a cloth for 15 to 20 minutes, and keeping it raised help the swelling.
explain: Running it off is a myth; carrying on playing on a sprain can make it worse.
fact: A sprain means the ligaments at a joint have been stretched or torn a little.
fact: A strain means some fibres in a muscle have been overstretched or torn; most strains heal in one to three weeks; rest it first, then move it gently, but do not stretch it hard.
fact: Cold narrows blood vessels and slows swelling and pain in the first day or two after an injury.
fact: Ice or frozen peas should be wrapped in a cloth and used for about 15 to 20 minutes at a time, because ice on bare skin can burn.
fact: A bruise is blood leaked from tiny broken blood vessels, and it turns from purple to blue, green and yellow as it heals.
fact: Bruises on shins and knees are normal in active children; lots of bruises in odd places, or bruises someone caused, should be told to a trusted adult; pressing or rubbing a bruise does not make it heal faster.
fact: The funny bone is not a bone but a nerve running over the elbow close to the skin, on its way to the little finger.
fact: A black eye spreads easily because the skin round the eye is thin and loose; a raw steak is a film joke, not a treatment.
fact: Growing pains are aches in both legs in the evening or night in children aged about 3 to 12; nobody is sure of the cause, but it is not bones growing too fast; leg pain with swelling, a limp or a fever is not growing pains and needs a doctor.
fact: A jammed finger should not be pulled hard; one that looks bent or will not straighten needs an X-ray.
fact: If you cannot walk on a twisted ankle at all, a doctor should check it.
fact: After a sprain the ankle can feel wobbly, because the stretched ligaments are looser and the nerves that sense the joint are confused, and balance exercises such as standing on one leg train it again.
exercise: What is a sprain? | Ligaments at a joint stretched or torn a little | A small crack in one of the bones of the ankle | A bruise on top of the foot from a knock | A muscle that has gone to sleep and gone numb for a while
exercise: Why put a bag of frozen peas on a hurt knee? | Cold slows down swelling and pain | Peas have a medicine in them that soaks into skin | Cold makes the knee bone knit back faster | It stops the knee from bending so it can rest
exercise: How should ice be used on an injury? | Wrapped in a cloth for about 15 to 20 minutes | Straight on bare skin all night long so it works | Only after a whole week has passed | Rubbed hard over the joint until the skin goes numb
exercise: What is a strain? | Muscle fibres overstretched or torn a little | A joint that has popped right out of its place | A thin crack running along a bone | Blood that has leaked under the skin
exercise: What is a bruise? | Blood leaked from tiny broken vessels under the skin | Skin that has been stained dark by the knock itself | A bone poking up near the surface after a bump | A small infection that sits in the top layer of skin
exercise: Why does a bruise turn green and yellow? | The body is breaking down and clearing the leaked blood | The bruise is getting infected and filling with germs | The skin is slowly going mouldy underneath | Fresh new blood is leaking in from the bone
exercise: Your friend has lots of bruises and seems scared at home. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult; it is not telling tales | Keep it a secret for ever, because he asked you to | Laugh about it with the others at break | Ask him to show the class so they can help
exercise: What is your funny bone? | A nerve that runs over the elbow | A small extra bone hidden inside the elbow | The knobbly end of the upper arm bone | A muscle in the arm that makes you laugh
exercise: What helps a black eye? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth | A slice of raw steak on the eye | Rubbing it gently with butter twice a day | A hot water bottle held against it
exercise: What are growing pains? | Harmless night aches in both legs of children | Bones growing too fast and stretching the skin | A sign that a leg bone is broken | Aches that only grown-ups get after work
exercise: What should you do with a jammed finger? | Use something cold and get it checked if it looks bent | Pull it hard straight away to put it back in place | Bend it backwards a few times to test it | Ignore it, tape it up and keep playing the match
exercise: Why does the ankle feel wobbly after a sprain? | Loose ligaments and confused joint-sensing nerves | The bone inside has gone soft from the twist | The ankle has shrunk and is too small now | The foot has forgotten how to walk for ever and must relearn
exercise: What helps a wobbly ankle after a sprain? | Balance exercises like standing on one leg | Never walking on that foot again, even when healed | Wearing two thick socks on that foot | Running a whole marathon right away to toughen it
exercise: Should you stretch a freshly pulled muscle hard? | No, rest first, then move it gently | Yes, stretch it as hard as you can straight away | Yes, a hard pull pops the muscle back | No, never use that muscle again
exercise: Which needs a doctor after a twisted ankle? | Not being able to walk on it at all | A small bit of swelling that goes down | Feeling a bit sore the next morning | Wanting to sit down for a while
test: coach yelled "run it off!" when I twisted me ankle. right call? | No, playing on a sprain can make it worse | Yes, running it off fixes sprains every time | Yes, if you run fast enough it's fine | No, you gotta jump on it instead
test: Ruby put ice cubes straight on her bare knee for an hour. What's the problem? | Ice on bare skin for long can cause a cold burn | Ice on a knee makes the bruise come out much bigger | Ice has no effect on a knee at all | Ice turns the knee green after an hour or so
test: My mate says pressing your bruise makes it go away faster. Right? | No, pressing on a bruise does not speed healing | Yes, pressing pushes the old blood away quicker | Yes, three hard presses a day does it | No, you have to rub it with salt to make it go
test: Banged my elbow and my little finger went all tingly, lol it's not funny! Why the tingle? | You knocked a nerve that runs to the little finger | You cracked the elbow bone and it's leaking | Your blood stopped flowing to that hand for a bit | Your finger fell asleep from boredom
test: In a cartoon, a boxer slaps a steak on his black eye. What should he really use? | A cold pack wrapped in cloth | A slice of cheese from the fridge | A hot towel to warm the eye up | Nothing, black eyes need surgery
test: Grandma says Ben's night leg aches are his bones growing too fast. What's closer to the truth? | Growing pains are common and harmless, but the cause isn't bones growing | Yes, she's right, bones grow mostly at night and that stretching is what hurts | Ben's legs must be broken if they ache | It means he needs a cast on both legs for a while
test: If a child had leg pain with swelling, a limp and a fever, what should happen? | A doctor should check, as it is not just growing pains | Nothing, it is ordinary growing pains and will pass | Rub it every night and wait a year to see | Give them ice cream and an early night
test: Netball hit my fingertip, now it won't straighten. Just yank it? | No, it needs an X-ray; pulling can make a break worse | Yes, one hard yank always fixes it | Yes, but only twice, then leave it | No, tape it flat to your nose till it straightens
test: Which is the right difference? | A sprain hurts ligaments; a strain hurts muscle | A sprain hurts muscle; a strain hurts the bone itself | They are both just names for broken bones | A strain is only a big bruise
test: What would happen if you ran hard straight after a strain? | You could tear the muscle more | It would heal faster from the extra blood | It would turn into a bruise instead | Nothing, a muscle cannot be hurt twice in a row
test: Why is a bruise first purple? | It is fresh blood under the skin | It is dye soaked in from your clothes | It is the bone showing through the skin | It is a patch of dead skin on the top
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a sprain? | An injury in which the ligaments of a joint are stretched or partially torn | A fracture of the bone adjacent to a joint, with no injury to soft tissue | An injury confined to the fibres of a muscle | A collection of blood beneath the skin following a blow
exam: Identify the recommended manner of applying cold to a recent injury. | Wrapped in cloth for some fifteen to twenty minutes at a time | Applied directly to the skin without interruption overnight | Deferred until at least one week after the injury | Applied with vigorous rubbing of the joint until the skin is numb
exam: State the principal cause of the discoloration of a bruise. | Blood escaped from minute vessels beneath the skin | Pigment transferred to the skin from clothing | Infection of the superficial layers of the skin | Displacement of the underlying bone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called funny bone. | It is a nerve passing superficially over the elbow | It is a small supplementary bone of the elbow joint | It is the extremity of the forearm bone | It is a muscle of the arm associated with laughter
